{"lines": ["So who was listening? The United States Navy.", "It built a chain of underwater microphones called the Sound Surveillance System, SOSUS, to track Soviet submarines. Its name and acronym were classified. Its cover name was Project Caesar. And its shore stations were described to the public as oceanographic research.", "The first experimental array went in off Eleuthera, in the Bahamas, in 1951. By 1952, a working array of forty hydrophones. And on the sixth of July, 1962, it made its first detection of a Soviet nuclear submarine, passing through the gap between Greenland, Iceland and the United Kingdom.", "The mission was declassified in 1991. In 1992, after the Cold War, the Navy partly opened its recordings to scientists. And NOAA's Pacific Marine Environmental Laboratory started recording in August of 1991.", "Rule two. Ears built for war can become ears for science."], "words": [["So", 0.0, 0.18], ["who", 0.18, 0.38], ["was", 0.38, 0.52], ["listening?", 0.52, 0.9], ["The", 1.26, 1.32], ["United", 1.32, 1.6], ["States", 1.6, 2.02], ["Navy.", 2.02, 2.42], ["It", 3.5, 3.58], ["built", 3.58, 3.76], ["a", 3.76, 3.92], ["chain", 3.92, 4.22], ["of", 4.22, 4.4], ["underwater", 4.4, 4.8], ["microphones", 4.8, 5.5], ["called", 5.5, 5.94], ["the", 5.94, 6.08], ["Sound", 6.08, 6.32], ["Surveillance", 6.32, 6.7], ["System,", 6.7, 7.32], ["SOSUS,", 7.32, 8.32], ["to", 8.32, 8.37], ["track", 8.37, 8.58], ["Soviet", 8.58, 9.02], ["submarines.", 9.02, 9.38], ["Its", 10.36, 10.41], ["name", 10.41, 10.64], ["and", 10.64, 10.9], ["acronym", 10.9, 11.24], ["were", 11.24, 11.6], ["classified.", 11.6, 11.86], ["Its", 12.76, 12.88], ["cover", 12.88, 13.1], ["name", 13.1, 13.4], ["was", 13.4, 13.62], ["Project", 13.62, 13.92], ["Caesar.", 13.92, 14.34], ["And", 14.9, 15.02], ["its", 15.02, 15.16], ["shore", 15.16, 15.36], ["stations", 15.36, 15.62], ["were", 15.62, 16.16], ["described", 16.16, 16.44], ["to", 16.44, 16.78], ["the", 16.78, 16.86], ["public", 16.86, 17.12], ["as